{"step_index":1931,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T07:08:22Z","input_tokens":5347,"cache_read_tokens":137454,"output_tokens":4414,"content":"# DSA Learning Library Inside \"Teach Me This Code\" — Phase 1 Complete\n\nI have implemented **Phase 1: Create the DSA Learning Dashboard & Curated 300 Problem Library** for the application without modifying the core execution engine (`engine.js`), without modifying the original Python code used by the visualizer, and without introducing any AI APIs, paid services, or external database dependencies. All progress tracking is **100% client-side, local, and real**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary of Changes & New Files\n\n| File | Change Type | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| [index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/index.html) | Modified | Added top-bar navigation switcher (`👁️ Code Visualizer` ↔ `📚 DSA Learning`), wrapped the visualizer workspace in `#visualizerViewContainer`, added the active problem banner (`#dsaProblemActiveBanner`), added `#dsaLearningContainer`, and included the new script modules. |\n| [styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/styles.css) | Modified | Appended comprehensive styling for view switcher tabs, hero continue learning card, metrics cards, difficulty progress bars, 17 topic mastery cards, 8-phase roadmap timeline, interactive problem table, filter pills, search bar, and problem details modal. |\n| [dsa_problems.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/dsa_problems.js) | Created | Curated library containing **exactly 300 distinct, identifiable DSA problems** (real LeetCode numbers, titles, patterns, descriptions, time/space complexities, and runnable PEP 8 indented Python code). |\n| [dsa_dashboard.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/dsa_dashboard.js) | Created | Main DSA application controller: manages `localStorage` progress persistence, metrics calculation, continue learning recommendations, recent history, 8-phase roadmap rendering, real-time search/filtering/sorting, and seamless loading into the code visualizer. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Curated 300 DSA Problems Library Breakdown\n\nThe library (`window.DSA_PROBLEMS_LIBRARY`) contains **300 distinct, verified problems** categorized across 17 essential algorithmic topics with real LeetCode numbers, patterns, and complexity bounds:\n\n### Difficulty Distribution\n* **Easy:** 78 problems (26.0%)\n* **Medium:** 194 problems (64.7%)\n* **Hard:** 28 problems (9.3%)\n* **Total:** **300 problems**\n\n### Topic Breakdown (All 17 Core Topics)\n1. **Arrays & Hashing (30 problems):** Two Sum (#1), Valid Anagram (#242), Group Anagrams (#49), Top K Frequent (#347), Product Except Self (#238), Longest Consecutive (#128), Subarray Sum Equals K (#560), Next Permutation (#31), etc.\n2. **Two Pointers (20 problems):** Two Sum II (#167), 3Sum (#15), 4Sum (#18), Container With Most Water (#11), Trapping Rain Water (#42), Move Zeroes (#283), Sort Colors (#75), etc.\n3. **Sliding Window (18 problems):** Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock (#121), Longest Substring Without Repeating (#3), Minimum Window Substring (#76), Sliding Window Maximum (#239), Permutation in String (#567), etc.\n4. **Stack (20 problems):** Valid Parentheses (#20), Min Stack (#155), Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation (#150), Daily Temperatures (#739), Largest Rectangle in Histogram (#84), Asteroid Collision (#735), etc.\n5. **Binary Search (20 problems):** Binary Search (#704), Search a 2D Matrix (#74), Koko Eating Bananas (#875), Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array (#153), Search in Rotated Array (#33), Median of Two Sorted Arrays (#4), etc.\n6. **Linked List (22 problems):** Reverse Linked List (#206), Merge Two Sorted Lists (#21), Reorder List (#143), Remove Nth Node (#19), Linked List Cycle (#141), Merge K Sorted Lists (#23), LRU Cache (#146), etc.\n7. **Trees & BST (32 problems):** Invert Binary Tree (#226), Maximum Depth (#104), Diameter (#543), Balanced Tree (#110), Same Tree (#100), Subtree (#572), Lowest Common Ancestor (#235), Binary Tree Level Order (#102), Validate BST (#98), Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree (#297), etc.\n8. **Tries (8 problems):** Implement Trie (#208), Design Add and Search Words (#211), Word Search II (#212), Extra Characters in a String (#2707), etc.\n9. **Heap / Priority Queue (16 problems):** Kth Largest Element in an Array (#215), Task Scheduler (#621), Design Twitter (#355), Find Median from Data Stream (#295), Last Stone Weight (#1046), etc.\n10. **Backtracking (18 problems):** Subsets (#78), Combination Sum (#39), Permutations (#46), Word Search (#79), Palindrome Partitioning (#131), N-Queens (#51), Letter Combinations (#17), etc.\n11. **Graphs (24 problems):** Number of Islands (#200), Clone Graph (#133), Pacific Atlantic Water Flow (#417), Surrounded Regions (#130), Rotting Oranges (#994), Course Schedule (#207), Word Ladder (#127), etc.\n12. **Advanced Graphs (10 problems):** Reconstruct Itinerary (#332), Min Cost to Connect All Points (#1584), Network Delay Time (#743), Swim in Rising Water (#778), Cheapest Flights Within K Stops (#787), etc.\n13. **1-D Dynamic Programming (24 problems):** Climbing Stairs (#70), Min Cost Climbing Stairs (#746), House Robber (#198), House Robber II (#213), Longest Palindromic Substring (#5), Decode Ways (#91), Coin Change (#322), Maximum Subarray / Kadane (#53), Longest Increasing Subsequence (#300), Word Break (#139), Partition Equal Subset Sum (#416), etc.\n14. **2-D Dynamic Programming (18 problems):** Unique Paths (#62), Longest Common Subsequence (#1143), Best Time with Cooldown (#309), Coin Change II (#518), Target Sum (#494), Edit Distance (#72), Burst Balloons (#312), Regular Expression Matching (#10), etc.\n15. **Greedy (10 problems):** Jump Game (#55), Jump Game II (#45), Gas Station (#134), Hand of Straights (#846), Merge Triplets (#1899), Partition Labels (#763), Valid Parenthesis String (#678), etc.\n16. **Intervals (4 problems):** Insert Interval (#57), Merge Intervals (#56), Non-overlapping Intervals (#435), Meeting Rooms II (#253).\n17. **Bit Manipulation (6 problems):** Single Number (#136), Number of 1 Bits (#191), Counting Bits (#338), Reverse Bits (#190), Missing Number (#268), Single Number III (#260).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Features Implemented in Phase 1\n\n### A. Navigation & View Switcher\n* The top bar now features a pill switcher:\n  * **`👁️ Code Visualizer`**: Displays the existing 3-area workspace (code editor, step-by-step teacher, memory visualizer, execution dry-run history, prediction quiz, and speed controls).\n  * **`📚 DSA Learning`**: Opens the new DSA Learning Dashboard.\n* Switching between views preserves execution state and variable history.\n\n### B. Live Progress & Real LocalStorage State\n* Real progress data stored locally in browser `localStorage` (`dsa_learning_progress_v1`):\n  * `solved`: Array of problem IDs marked solved.\n  * `attempted`: Array of problem IDs opened and stepped through in the visualizer.\n  * `bookmarked`: Array of starred problems saved for review.\n  * `history`: Chronological activity log with problem ID, timestamp, and action.\n  * `lastPracticedId`: Pointer to the most recently engaged problem.\n* **Zero Fake Numbers or Fake Streaks**: Initial state starts at exactly **0 Solved (0.0%)**, **0 Attempted**, **300 Not Started**, **0 Bookmarked**. Every number is calculated strictly from saved data.\n\n### C. Dashboard Metrics & Visual Distribution\n1. **5 Metrics Cards**: Total Curated Problems (300), Solved Count (with % completion), In Progress / Attempted Count, Not Started Count, and Bookmarked Count.\n2. **\"Continue Learning\" Hero Card**:\n   * If a problem was recently worked on: displays `#<LC_NUM> Title`, difficulty, pattern, topic, and a **`▶ Continue Visualizing`** button.\n   * If no problem has been started yet: guides complete beginners to start with **`#1 Two Sum`**.\n3. **Difficulty Breakdown**:\n   * Interactive progress bars for **Easy (78)**, **Medium (194)**, and **Hard (28)**.\n   * Clicking any difficulty filters the problem table automatically.\n4. **Recently Practiced Problems**:\n   * Displays recent sessions with formatted relative time (\"Just now\", \"5m ago\"), difficulty tag, topic, status, and direct launch button.\n5. **Topic Mastery Grid**:\n   * 17 cards with custom icons, color indicators, problem counts, solved percentages, and click-to-filter capability.\n6. **Beginner Roadmap (8 Structured Phases)**:\n   * **Phase 1:** Core Fundamentals & Hash Tables (*Arrays & Hashing*)\n   * **Phase 2:** Sequential Pointers & Sliding Windows (*Two Pointers, Sliding Window*)\n   * **Phase 3:** Linear State & Monotonic Data Structures (*Stack, Linked List*)\n   * **Phase 4:** Divide-and-Conquer & Space Pruning (*Binary Search*)\n   * **Phase 5:** Hierarchical Structures & Priority Queues (*Trees, Tries, Heaps*)\n   * **Phase 6:** Exhaustive Search & State-Space Backtracking (*Backtracking*)\n   * **Phase 7:** Network Traversal, DAGs & Connectivity (*Graphs, Advanced Graphs*)\n   * **Phase 8:** Optimal Substructure & Dynamic Programming (*1D/2D DP, Greedy, Intervals, Bits*)\n   * Each phase displays learning goals, milestone problems, completion metrics, and a direct jump to that phase's problems.\n\n### D. Problem Library Explorer (Search, Filter, Sort, Pagination)\n* **Real-time Search:** Filters across titles, LeetCode numbers, patterns, topics, or descriptions.\n* **Filter Pills:** Filter by Topic (17 topics), Difficulty (All, Easy, Medium, Hard), and Status (All, Solved, Attempted, Not Started, Bookmarked).\n* **Sorting:** By LeetCode # (Ascending/Descending), Difficulty (Easy → Hard), or Title (A → Z).\n* **Problem Table:** Displays star toggle, LC badge, title with pattern chip, topic tag, difficulty badge, status pill, time/space complexity, and a **`▶ Solve & Step`** button.\n* **Pagination:** Smooth pagination (25 problems per page) with page numbers and Prev/Next navigation.\n* **Problem Detail Modal:** Clicking any problem title opens a clean modal with full description, algorithm pattern explanation, complexity analysis, code preview, and direct launch button.\n\n### E. Visualizer Pairing\n* Clicking **`▶ Solve & Step`** on any problem:\n  1. Marks the problem as `attempted` and appends to practice history in `localStorage`.\n  2. Sets `lastPracticedId`.\n  3. Injects the runnable Python code into the visualizer editor.\n  4. Displays an active DSA banner at the top of the visualizer:\n     * *`📚 Practicing DSA: #<LC> <Title> [<Difficulty> • <Topic> • Pattern: <Pattern>]`*\n     * Features a **`✓ Mark as Solved`** button and a **`← Back to DSA Dashboard`** button.\n  5. Automatically runs code execution and switches smoothly to the Code Visualizer view.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Verification & Testing Performed\n\n### Test Suite 1: Phase 1 DSA Library & Dashboard Audit (`test_dsa_phase1.js`)\n* Executed via Node.js: **59 / 59 tests passed (100% success)**.\n  * Verified DOM structure and static file inclusion.\n  * Verified all 300 problems exist with unique IDs, unique titles, valid fields, and proper difficulty/topic distribution.\n  * Verified real `localStorage` persistence and metric computation (zero fake numbers).\n  * Verified bookmarking, status transitions (`not_started` → `attempted` → `solved`), and history logging.\n  * Verified multi-criteria search, topic filtering, difficulty filtering, status filtering, and sorting.\n  * Verified execution of curated Python solutions on `engine.js` across core patterns:\n    * LeetCode 1 (Two Sum) — `ok=true`, 13 execution steps\n    * LeetCode 53 (Maximum Subarray / Kadane) — `ok=true`, 49 execution steps\n    * LeetCode 704 (Binary Search) — `ok=true`, 13 execution steps\n    * LeetCode 121 (Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock) — `ok=true`, 27 execution steps\n    * LeetCode 11 (Container With Most Water) — `ok=true`, 54 execution steps\n\n### Test Suite 2: Regression Audit on Existing Visualizer (`test_phase5.js`)\n* Executed via Node.js: **82 / 82 tests passed (100% success)**.\n  * All 5 test datasets (Kadane original, all-negative, all-positive, single-negative, single-positive) verified.\n  * Kadane maximum subarray output verified: **output remains 6** for input `[-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]`.\n  * Forward and backward navigation across 197 substeps verified.\n  * Variable memory addresses, interactive prediction quiz questions, and animation speed controls preserved.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Next Steps\n\nPhase 1 is complete and verified. Ready to proceed whenever you'd like to begin **Phase 2 (e.g., Problem Solving Workflow & Pattern Guides)** or test the interface live.","thinking":"The visualizer's HTML structure now includes new elements: view switcher tabs, a visualizer container, a DSA banner, and a learning container, along with updated script tags. CSS styles were also appended to comprehensively support a Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) Academy. Finally, a new JavaScript file was added containing 300 curated DSA problems.\n\n\nA new DSA learning dashboard was implemented, leveraging local storage for progress tracking and includes metrics computation, continue learning functionality, recent history, an 8-phase roadmap, search/filter/sorting features, a problem explorer, and visualizer integration. The core engine remains untouched. Existing application modules (`app.js`, `explainer.js`) were preserved. All existing and new tests pass successfully, confirming that the implementation adheres to the specified requirements.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":1932,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T07:13:09Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n4. Phase 2: Organize Problems by Topic\n\nCreate a searchable topic library covering:\n\nArrays\n\nStrings\n\nHashing and Hash Maps\n\nTwo Pointers\n\nSliding Window\n\nPrefix Sum\n\nBinary Search\n\nSorting\n\nLinked Lists\n\nStacks\n\nQueues and Deques\n\nRecursion\n\nBacktracking\n\nTrees and Binary Trees\n\nBinary Search Trees\n\nHeaps and Priority Queues\n\nGraphs\n\nBreadth-First Search (BFS)\n\nDepth-First Search (DFS)\n\nGreedy Algorithms\n\nDynamic Programming\n\nBit Manipulation\n\nTries\n\nUnion-Find\n\nTopological Sorting\n\nIntervals\n\nMonotonic Stack and Queue\n\nMath and Number Theory\n\nMatrix Problems\n\nAdvanced Data Structures\n\nProblems may belong to multiple topics.\n\nEach topic page should display its problem count, difficulty distribution, completion progress, and recommended next problem.\n\nAllow users to browse by topic, difficulty, algorithmic pattern, and learning status.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-27T12:43:09+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":1933,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T07:13:09Z","input_tokens":4658,"cache_read_tokens":142969,"output_tokens":6171,"thinking":"Initial task is to create a searchable topic library. The library must cover the specified data structures and algorithms. Need to ensure all items are included and organized in a way that allows for efficient searching.\n\n\nThe 30 topics must be incorporated into the searchable library. Recognizing the need for multi-topic assignment, I will change the problem data structure to support multiple topic associations per problem. This likely involves updating the problem data schema.\n\n\nConsidering example problems, each problem is associated with multiple topics. The data structure must accommodate a list or set of topics for each problem. This suggests a many-to-many relationship between problems and topics. Therefore, the data schema needs to be updated to reflect this relationship.\n\n\nEach problem's association with multiple topics confirms the need for a many-to-many relationship in the data structure. The current focus is on designing the \"Topic Library\" view, including a quick-filter search bar for easy topic navigation. The library will display each topic as a card, showing topic name, icon, total problem count, and completion progress.\n\n\nThe \"Topic Library\" view will include difficulty distribution badges and a recommended next problem for each topic card. The dedicated topic page will present a comprehensive view, encompassing a header with topic details, statistical summaries, a difficulty distribution visualization, and a \"Recommended Next Problem\" card.\n\nWithin each topic, filtering and browsing controls will allow sorting by difficulty, algorithmic pattern, learning status, search functionality, and sort options (LC#, Difficulty, Title). The problem list will display problems with various attributes. Each includes a bookmark, LC# identifier, title with algorithmic patterns, other topic tags, difficulty, status toggle, complexity metrics, and a direct \"Solve & Step\" button.\n\n\nThe problem data structure will be modified to include a `topics` array, accommodating the specified 30 topics for each problem. This multi-topic association is crucial for the browsable topic view and effective search. Initial inspection of project files shows a clear path for data integration.\n\n\nNow analyzing the provided topic list to determine the appropriate topics for the 300 problems. Considering a script that uses problem details (title, description, code) for topic association. The goal is to generate a comprehensive `topics` array for each problem, incorporating all applicable topics from the list. This array will facilitate the creation of searchable and browsable topic views. The examples provide good guidance.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"import json\nimport re\n\nproblems_path = r\"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer\\app_build\\dsa_problems.js\"\n\nwith open(problems_path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    text = f.read()\n\nprefix = \"window.DSA_PROBLEMS_LIBRARY = \"\nstart_idx = text.find(prefix) + len(prefix)\nend_idx = text.rfind(\"];\") + 1\nraw_json = text[start_idx:end_idx]\nproblems = json.loads(raw_json)\n\nprint(f\"Loaded {len(problems)} problems\")\n\n# List of 30 official topics specified by the user\nALL_30_TOPICS = [\n    \"Arrays\",\n    \"Strings\",\n    \"Hashing and Hash Maps\",\n    \"Two Pointers\",\n    \"Sliding Window\",\n    \"Prefix Sum\",\n    \"Binary Search\",\n    \"Sorting\",\n    \"Linked Lists\",\n    \"Stacks\",\n    \"Queues and Deques\",\n    \"Recursion\",\n    \"Backtracking\",\n    \"Trees and Binary Trees\",\n    \"Binary Search Trees\",\n    \"Heaps and Priority Queues\",\n    \"Graphs\",\n    \"Breadth-First Search (BFS)\",\n    \"Depth-First Search (DFS)\",\n    \"Greedy Algorithms\",\n    \"Dynamic Programming\",\n    \"Bit Manipulation\",\n    \"Tries\",\n    \"Union-Find\",\n    \"Topological Sorting\",\n    \"Intervals\",\n    \"Monotonic Stack and Queue\",\n    \"Math and Number Theory\",\n    \"Matrix Problems\",\n    \"Advanced Data Structures\"\n]\n\n# Build rule-based multi-topic tagger for each problem based on LC number, title, description, code, pattern, and original topic\ndef assign_topics(p):\n    topics = set()\n    lc = p[\"leetcodeNumber\"]\n    title = p[\"title\"].lower()\n    desc = p[\"description\"].lower()\n    code = p[\"starterCode\"].lower()\n    pattern = p[\"pattern\"].lower()\n    orig = p.get(\"topic\", \"\")\n\n    # Base topic mapping from Phase 1\n    if \"Array\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Arrays\")\n    if \"Hash\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Hashing and Hash Maps\")\n    if \"Two Pointer\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Two Pointers\")\n        topics.add(\"Arrays\")\n    if \"Sliding Window\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Sliding Window\")\n        topics.add(\"Arrays\")\n    if \"Stack\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Stacks\")\n    if \"Binary Search\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Binary Search\")\n    if \"Linked List\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Linked Lists\")\n    if \"Tree\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Trees and Binary Trees\")\n    if \"Trie\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Tries\")\n        topics.add(\"Trees and Binary Trees\")\n    if \"Heap\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Heaps and Priority Queues\")\n    if \"Backtracking\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Backtracking\")\n        topics.add(\"Recursion\")\n    if \"Graph\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Graphs\")\n    if \"Dynamic Programming\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Dynamic Programming\")\n    if \"Greedy\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Greedy Algorithms\")\n    if \"Interval\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Intervals\")\n        topics.add(\"Arrays\")\n    if \"Bit\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Bit Manipulation\")\n\n    # String identification\n    if any(k in title for k in [\"string\", \"anagram\", \"parentheses\", \"palindrome\", \"word\", \"subsequence\", \"decode\", \"roman\", \"prefix\", \"character\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"string\", \"character\", \"substring\", \"anagram\", \"palindrome\", \"words\", \"letters\"]):\n        topics.add(\"Strings\")\n\n    # Arrays identification\n    if any(k in title for k in [\"array\", \"subarrays\", \"matrix\", \"nums\", \"numbers\", \"duplicate\", \"sum\", \"stock\", \"product\", \"element\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"array\", \"list\", \"integers\", \"matrix\", \"elements\", \"grid\"]):\n        topics.add(\"Arrays\")\n\n    # Hashing & Hash Maps\n    if any(k in code for k in [\"{\", \"dict\", \"map\", \"seen\", \"freq\", \"count\", \"lookup\", \"memo\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"hash\", \"frequency\", \"count\", \"map\", \"unique\", \"duplicate\", \"seen\"]) or \\\n       any(k in pattern for k in [\"hash\", \"frequency\", \"map\", \"lookup\", \"counter\"]):\n        topics.add(\"Hashing and Hash Maps\")\n\n    # Prefix Sum\n    if any(k in pattern for k in [\"prefix sum\", \"cumulative\", \"running sum\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"prefix sum\", \"cumulative sum\", \"subarray sum\"]) or \\\n       \"prefix\" in title or lc in [560, 525, 238, 303, 304, 724, 974, 1248, 523]:\n        topics.add(\"Prefix Sum\")\n        topics.add(\"Arrays\")\n\n    # Sorting\n    if any(k in code for k in [\".sort(\", \"sorted(\", \"sort\"]) or \\\n       any(k in pattern for k in [\"sort\", \"partition\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"sorted\", \"sort the\", \"order\"]):\n        topics.add(\"Sorting\")\n\n    # Queues & Deques\n    if any(k in code for k in [\"deque\", \"queue\", \"popleft\", \"appendleft\"]) or \\\n       any(k in pattern for k in [\"queue\", \"deque\", \"bfs\", \"level order\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"queue\", \"deque\", \"fifo\", \"level order\"]):\n        topics.add(\"Queues and Deques\")\n\n    # Monotonic Stack & Queue\n    if any(k in pattern for k in [\"monotonic stack\", \"monotonic queue\", \"next greater\", \"next smaller\"]) or \\\n       lc in [739, 84, 85, 42, 496, 503, 901, 239, 402, 316, 1762]:\n        topics.add(\"Monotonic Stack and Queue\")\n        topics.add(\"Stacks\")\n\n    # Recursion\n    if \"Recursion\" in orig or \"def \" in code and code.count(p.get(\"title\", \"\").split()[0].lower()) > 1 or \\\n       any(k in pattern for k in [\"recursive\", \"dfs\", \"backtrack\", \"divide and conquer\", \"tree dfs\"]) or \\\n       \"recursion\" in desc or \"recursive\" in desc:\n        topics.add(\"Recursion\")\n\n    # Binary Search Trees (BST)\n    if \"bst\" in title or \"binary search tree\" in title or \"bst\" in desc or \"binary search tree\" in desc or \\\n       lc in [98, 235, 700, 701, 450, 230, 108, 173, 538, 669]:\n        topics.add(\"Binary Search Trees\")\n        topics.add(\"Trees and Binary Trees\")\n\n    # Breadth-First Search (BFS)\n    if any(k in pattern for k in [\"bfs\", \"level order\", \"shortest path\", \"queue traversal\", \"multi-source bfs\"]) or \\\n       any(k in code for k in [\"deque\", \"popleft\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"level by level\", \"bfs\", \"breadth-first\", \"shortest path\"]) or \\\n       lc in [102, 107, 103, 111, 200, 994, 127, 752, 1091, 542, 207]:\n        topics.add(\"Breadth-First Search (BFS)\")\n        topics.add(\"Queues and Deques\")\n\n    # Depth-First Search (DFS)\n    if any(k in pattern for k in [\"dfs\", \"depth-first\", \"tree traversal\", \"graph dfs\", \"postorder\", \"inorder\", \"preorder\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"dfs\", \"depth-first\", \"traverse\"]) or \\\n       \"Trees\" in orig or \"Graphs\" in orig or \"Backtracking\" in orig:\n        topics.add(\"Depth-First Search (DFS)\")\n\n    # Union-Find\n    if any(k in pattern for k in [\"union find\", \"disjoint set\", \"connected component\"]) or \\\n       any(k in code for k in [\"find(p)\", \"union(p\", \"parent\", \"find(x)\"]) or \\\n       lc in [200, 684, 685, 547, 721, 1319, 128, 778, 1584, 261, 323]:\n        topics.add(\"Union-Find\")\n        topics.add(\"Graphs\")\n\n    # Topological Sorting\n    if any(k in pattern for k in [\"topological sort\", \"kahn\", \"dependency resolution\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"prerequisites\", \"topological order\", \"cycle in directed\", \"dag\"]) or \\\n       lc in [207, 210, 269, 802, 1857, 1136]:\n        topics.add(\"Topological Sorting\")\n        topics.add(\"Graphs\")\n\n    # Matrix Problems\n    if any(k in title for k in [\"matrix\", \"grid\", \"spiral\", \"island\", \"board\", \"surrounded\", \"rotting\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"2d grid\", \"matrix\", \"m x n\", \"grid\", \"rows and columns\", \"row and column\"]) or \\\n       any(k in code for k in [\"grid[r][c]\", \"matrix[r][c]\", \"matrix[i][j]\", \"len(matrix)\"]) or \\\n       lc in [73, 54, 48, 74, 79, 200, 130, 417, 994, 1091, 62, 63, 64]:\n        topics.add(\"Matrix Problems\")\n        topics.add(\"Arrays\")\n\n    # Math and Number Theory\n    if any(k in title for k in [\"math\", \"sum\", \"roman\", \"integer\", \"number\", \"multiply\", \"divide\", \"pow\", \"sqrt\", \"ugly\", \"happy\"]) or \\\n       any(k in pattern for k in [\"math\", \"modular\", \"prime\", \"geometry\", \"simulation\"]) or \\\n       any(k in desc for k in [\"prime\", \"modulus\", \"factorial\", \"power of\", \"gcd\", \"lcm\", \"mathematical\"]) or \\\n       lc in [7, 9, 12, 13, 43, 50, 66, 69, 149, 172, 202, 204, 263, 264, 326, 342, 367, 504]:\n        topics.add(\"Math and Number Theory\")\n\n    # Advanced Data Structures\n    if any(k in pattern for k in [\"segment tree\", \"fenwick\", \"binary indexed tree\", \"trie\", \"lru\", \"lfu\", \"median finder\"]) or \\\n       lc in [146, 460, 295, 208, 211, 212, 307, 308, 355, 622, 641, 1584, 743, 778, 787]:\n        topics.add(\"Advanced Data Structures\")\n\n    # Clean and filter to official 30\n    final_topics = [t for t in ALL_30_TOPICS if t in topics]\n    # Ensure every problem has at least 1 topic\n    if not final_topics:\n        final_topics = [orig] if orig in ALL_30_TOPICS else [\"Arrays\"]\n    return sorted(final_topics)\n\ntopic_distribution = {t: 0 for t in ALL_30_TOPICS}\nfor p in problems:\n    p[\"topics\"] = assign_topics(p)\n    for t in p[\"topics\"]:\n        topic_distribution[t] += 1\n\nprint(\"\\n--- 30 TOPICS PROBLEM DISTRIBUTION ---\")\nfor t, count in topic_distribution.items():\n    print(f\"  {t:30}: {count} problems\")\n\nzero_topics = [t for t, count in topic_distribution.items() if count == 0]\nprint(f\"\\nTopics with 0 problems: {zero_topics}\")\nassert len(zero_topics) == 0, f\"Some topics have 0 problems: {zero_topics}\"\n","Description":"Inspect 30 topics distribution on 300 problems","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\4b927fe1-f9a3-4b3f-9f8a-92760e157eef\\scratch\\test_multi_topics.py","toolAction":"Testing multi-topic tagger","toolSummary":"Run test_multi_topics.py"}}]}
